# --- LLM-powered analysis (optional) ----------------------------------------
# agent-guard drives two scanners:
#   - SkillSpector   -> skill scans + the static MCP source scan
#   - cisco-ai-mcp-scanner -> the optional runtime MCP check (scan_mcp.py
#                             --sandbox / remote)
# Both can use an LLM for deeper semantic analysis. Configure ONE provider
# below; scan_mcp.py bridges your choice to the Cisco runtime scanner
# automatically, so you only set it once.
#
# No provider at all? That is fine: skill scans run with `--no-llm` and the
# MCP source scan falls back to static-only. You lose semantic analysis, not
# the scan.
#
# Note: verdict quality depends on model quality. For security decisions,
# prefer a capable model -- small local models weaken threat detection.
#
# These are SkillSpector's native variable names (SKILLSPECTOR_* + the
# provider's own key var). They are NOT the same as the old Cisco
# SKILL_SCANNER_* names; scan_mcp.py still reads SKILL_SCANNER_* as a legacy
# fallback, but new setups should use the variables below.
#
# SkillSpector LLM compatibility: its semantic analyzers only work with the
# OpenAI and NVIDIA providers. With Anthropic, skill scans and the static MCP
# scan run STATIC-ONLY (patterns, taint, YARA, OSV.dev CVE lookup -- still
# strong); the Anthropic key is then used only for the optional runtime MCP
# check (scan_mcp.py --sandbox / remote), which works with any provider via
# LiteLLM. To enable SkillSpector's LLM layer, pick OpenAI (Option B/C) or
# NVIDIA (Option D).

# --- Option A: Anthropic (powers the runtime MCP scan; SkillSpector static-only) ---
SKILLSPECTOR_PROVIDER=anthropic
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# claude-haiku is fast and cheap for scanning:
SKILLSPECTOR_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# --- Option B: OpenAI ---
# SKILLSPECTOR_PROVIDER=openai
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# SKILLSPECTOR_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# --- Option C: Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint ---
# (OpenRouter, Groq, Azure OpenAI, vLLM, LM Studio, local Ollama, ...)
# SkillSpector reaches these through its OpenAI provider + a base URL.
# SKILLSPECTOR_PROVIDER=openai
# OPENAI_API_KEY=...                          # any non-empty value for Ollama
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1   # e.g. local Ollama
# SKILLSPECTOR_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:32b

# --- Option D: NVIDIA build/inference (SkillSpector's default path) ---
# SKILLSPECTOR_PROVIDER=nv_inference          # or nv_build
# NVIDIA_INFERENCE_KEY=nvapi-...

# --- Optional: override the Cisco runtime MCP scanner (scan_mcp.py) ---
# Not needed normally: scan_mcp.py derives this from the provider you chose
# above. Set it only to use a DIFFERENT model for the runtime MCP check than
# for skill/static scans. Value is a LiteLLM model id (provider/model). The
# Cisco MCP scanner defaults to gpt-4o if neither this nor a usable provider
# above is set.
# MCP_SCANNER_LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5-20251001
# MCP_SCANNER_LLM_BASE_URL=     # only for Ollama / OpenAI-compatible overrides
